from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Gender studies is best described as...","options":["a single academic discipline with one fixed method","an interdisciplinary field drawing on many disciplines","a branch of biology","a political party"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The field combines questions, evidence, and methods from several disciplines, including sociology, history, and anthropology."},{"prompt":"In much scholarship, the distinction between sex and gender is that...","options":["they mean exactly the same thing","sex refers to social roles and gender to biology","sex refers to biological characteristics and gender to social roles and identities","neither term is used in the field"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A common academic usage links sex to biology and gender to socially learned roles and identities."},{"prompt":"To say that gender is socially constructed means...","options":["gender has no effect on people''s lives","many expectations tied to gender are produced by societies rather than fixed by nature","gender is reinvented by each person every morning","gender cannot be studied at all"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Social construction means the expectation is shaped by society; constructed things can still be very real in their effects."},{"prompt":"The term patriarchy, as scholars use it, refers to...","options":["a social system in which power and authority are concentrated in men","a country in southern Europe","the equal sharing of household labor","a type of statistical test"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In scholarship the term names a structure of power, not an accusation against particular individuals."},{"prompt":"Intersectionality, associated with Kimberle Crenshaw, describes...","options":["how roads cross in a city","how gender overlaps with race, class, and other categories to shape experience","a single underlying cause of all inequality","a method for grading essays"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Intersectionality analyzes how overlapping categories combine to produce distinct experiences."},{"prompt":"Gender studies grew most directly out of which earlier field?","options":["women''s studies","astronomy","accounting","civil engineering"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The field expanded from women''s studies, which took shape in universities in the late 1960s and 1970s."},{"prompt":"Gender roles are...","options":["parts that actors play in films","shared expectations about how people of a given gender should behave","laws passed by national governments","automatic biological reflexes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A gender role is a shared social expectation about behavior, dress, work, or feeling."},{"prompt":"Which of the following is a method commonly used in gender studies?","options":["only laboratory chemistry","interviews, ethnography, and textual analysis","only the polling of elections","the field uses no methods at all"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The field uses qualitative methods such as interviews and ethnography alongside surveys and textual analysis."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'What gender studies is: an interdisciplinary field', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to define gender studies in your own words, list several of the disciplines it draws upon, and explain why most scholars describe it as an interdisciplinary field rather than a single, self-contained subject. Gender studies asks how ideas about femininity, masculinity, and other gender categories shape individual lives and whole societies. It examines the beliefs, institutions, and everyday practices through which gender becomes meaningful, and it studies how those arrangements change over time and differ from place to place. Warm-up, drawing on the diagnostic quiz: when you were asked to describe the field, the best answer was not a single laboratory method but a meeting point of many approaches. Keep that image in mind. As you read, try to notice how one question about gender can be approached by a historian, a sociologist, a literary critic, and an anthropologist, each contributing a different and complementary piece of the picture."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Gender studies is the academic field that examines gender as a central feature of social life. It treats gender not merely as a personal attribute but as a pattern that organizes families, workplaces, laws, languages, and cultures. Because gender touches so many areas, no single discipline can capture it alone. Scholars therefore draw on sociology, history, anthropology, psychology, political science, literature, philosophy, and law, among others. This is what interdisciplinary means: the field combines the questions, evidence, and methods of several disciplines rather than relying on one. A sociologist might survey how housework is divided; a historian might trace how expectations for women changed across a century; a literary critic might analyze how novels portray masculinity. Gender studies is also closely related to, and sometimes houses, women''s studies, queer studies, and masculinity studies, which examine particular dimensions of the same broad subject. The field is established in universities around the world, with departments, journals, and professional associations. Its central commitment is analytical: to describe and explain how gender works, and to test claims against evidence, rather than to assume that current arrangements are simply natural or inevitable."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"Several figures helped define the field. Simone de Beauvoir, in her 1949 book The Second Sex, argued that one is not born but rather becomes a woman, suggesting that womanhood is shaped by society as much as by biology. The sociologist Ann Oakley, in Sex, Gender and Society (1972), drew a clear line between biological sex and socially learned gender, a distinction that became foundational. Later, the philosopher Judith Butler argued that gender is something people perform through repeated actions rather than a fixed inner essence. An example shows the interdisciplinary method at work. Suppose we ask why fewer women than men have historically become engineers. A psychologist might study how confidence develops; a sociologist might examine hiring and mentoring; a historian might show that engineering was defined over time as masculine; an economist might measure pay and promotion gaps. No single answer is complete, but together they build a fuller explanation. This is the pattern of gender studies: a shared question, examined with many tools. The field does not claim one cause for complex outcomes; it weighs evidence from several directions and remains open to revision as new research appears."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Try each question before reading the answer. 1) In one sentence, define gender studies. 2) Name three disciplines the field draws on. 3) What does the word interdisciplinary mean here? 4) Give an example of a question that a historian and a sociologist might approach differently. 5) Which 1949 book is often cited as an early influence on the field? Answers: 1) Gender studies is the interdisciplinary field that examines how gender shapes individuals and societies. 2) Any three of sociology, history, anthropology, psychology, political science, literature, philosophy, and law. 3) It means the field combines questions, evidence, and methods from several disciplines rather than one. 4) For example, why housework has been divided unequally: a historian traces how the pattern formed over time, while a sociologist measures who does what today. 5) The Second Sex, by Simone de Beauvoir."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating gender studies as a single method or a single political position. It is a broad field of inquiry with many internal debates and approaches. Mistake: assuming interdisciplinary means unfocused. The field has a clear focus, namely gender as a feature of social life, and simply uses several disciplines to study it. Mistake: confusing gender studies with activism. Although the field often engages social questions, its scholarly work rests on evidence, argument, and peer review, and researchers disagree with one another. Exit ticket: (a) Is gender studies one discipline or a meeting point of many? (b) Name one discipline it draws on. (c) True or false: because it is interdisciplinary, the field has no clear subject. Answers: (a) a meeting point of many disciplines; (b) any of sociology, history, anthropology, psychology, political science, literature, philosophy, or law; (c) false, since its subject is how gender shapes individuals and societies."}]}'::jsonb, 'Interdisciplinary means a field borrows concepts and methods from several disciplines at once; here it is a strength, not a lack of focus.', 12),
  (3, 'Sex, gender, and social construction', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to distinguish between the terms sex and gender as they are commonly used in scholarship, explain what it means to say that gender is socially constructed, and give evidence for that claim. You will also be able to describe why many researchers treat both categories as more complex than a simple division into two. Warm-up, from the diagnostic: when asked how sex and gender differ, the strongest answer linked sex to biological characteristics and gender to social roles and identities. Hold that working definition as you read, but be ready to refine it. The aim here is not to settle every debate but to give you precise vocabulary and a clear sense of what the social construction of gender does and does not mean. As you read, keep asking: what counts as evidence that an expectation is produced by society rather than fixed by nature?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"In everyday speech, sex and gender are often used as synonyms. In much academic writing they are distinguished. Sex usually refers to biological characteristics such as chromosomes, hormones, and anatomy. Gender usually refers to the social roles, behaviors, expectations, and identities that societies attach to being, for instance, a woman or a man. The sociologist Ann Oakley helped popularize this distinction in the 1970s. To say that gender is socially constructed is to say that many of these roles and expectations are produced and maintained by societies rather than dictated by biology. The strongest evidence is variation: expectations about what is masculine or feminine differ across cultures and shift over time. Clothing, occupations, colors, and manners once coded as one gender are coded differently elsewhere or in another era. Social construction does not mean gender is imaginary or trivial; constructed things, like money and law, are very real in their effects. It also does not deny biology. Most scholars describe an interaction between biological factors and social ones, and many stress that both sex and gender are more varied than a strict two-category model suggests."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"Simone de Beauvoir''s claim that one becomes a woman anticipated the idea that gender is learned. Ann Oakley gave the sex and gender distinction its influential sociological form. Candace West and Don Zimmerman later described doing gender, arguing that gender is accomplished in ordinary interactions rather than simply possessed. Judith Butler pushed further, describing gender as performative, that is, produced through repeated acts that come to seem natural. Consider some examples of variation that support the constructionist view. The color pink was marketed as a boy''s color in parts of the early twentieth century before the association reversed. Occupations such as clerical work and medicine have shifted in their gender coding across countries and decades. In some societies, additional gender categories beyond man and woman are long recognized. Each example shows an expectation changing while biology did not, which is strong evidence that the expectation is social. Critics and friendly interpreters alike debate how far construction extends and how biology and society interact; the field treats these as open empirical questions rather than settled conclusions, and good scholarship weighs competing explanations with care."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In common academic usage, what does sex refer to, and what does gender refer to? 2) State one piece of evidence that a gender expectation is socially constructed. 3) Does social construction mean gender is not real? 4) Who helped popularize the sex and gender distinction in sociology? 5) What did West and Zimmerman mean by doing gender? Answers: 1) Sex refers to biological characteristics such as chromosomes, hormones, and anatomy; gender refers to social roles, behaviors, and identities. 2) Any expectation that varies across cultures or changes over time while biology does not, such as which occupations or colors are coded as masculine or feminine. 3) No, since constructed things such as money and law are real and have powerful effects; it means the expectation is produced by society, not fixed by nature. 4) Ann Oakley. 5) That gender is accomplished through everyday interactions, not merely held as a trait."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: using sex and gender as perfect synonyms in academic writing, which blurs a distinction the field relies on. Mistake: hearing socially constructed and concluding that gender is fake or unimportant; constructed categories shape real opportunities and constraints. Mistake: assuming social construction denies biology; most scholars describe biology and society interacting. Mistake: treating the two-category model as the whole story; researchers note wider variation in both sex traits and gender identities. Exit ticket: (a) Which term usually points to biological traits, and which to social roles? (b) Give one example of cross-cultural or historical variation in gender expectations. (c) True or false: calling something socially constructed means it has no real effects. Answers: (a) sex points to biological traits, gender to social roles; (b) for example, occupations or colors coded differently across societies or eras; (c) false, since constructed categories have very real effects."}]}'::jsonb, 'Sex and gender are often distinguished in scholarship: sex points to biological traits, gender to social roles and identities; many writers also note that both categories are more complex than a simple two-way split.', 13),
  (4, 'Core concepts: roles, socialization, patriarchy, intersectionality', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to define four core concepts used throughout gender studies, namely gender roles, socialization, patriarchy, and intersectionality, and apply each to a concrete example. These terms recur across the readings that follow, so a firm grasp now will make later material clearer. Warm-up, from the diagnostic: you met gender roles as shared expectations about behavior and intersectionality as the overlap of gender with other categories such as race and class. This lesson sharpens those definitions and adds two more. As you read, treat each concept as an analytical tool. A good tool helps you notice patterns and ask better questions; it is not a verdict announced in advance. Keep asking what each concept lets you see that you might otherwise miss, and where it might be applied too loosely. By the end you should be able to explain each term to someone new and give a clear example of it in action."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Gender roles are the shared expectations a society holds about how people of a given gender should act, dress, work, and feel. Socialization is the lifelong process through which people learn these roles and the broader norms of their society, beginning in childhood through family, schooling, peers, and media. Patriarchy names a social system in which power, authority, and resources are concentrated in the hands of men, often expressed through laws, institutions, and customs; scholars use the term to describe a structure, not to accuse individuals. Intersectionality, a term introduced by the legal scholar Kimberle Crenshaw, describes how gender overlaps with race, class, sexuality, disability, and other categories to produce distinct experiences that cannot be understood by looking at any one category alone. A working-class woman and a wealthy woman, for instance, may share a gender yet face very different constraints. Together these four concepts let researchers connect the personal and the structural: roles and socialization describe how expectations are learned and lived, while patriarchy and intersectionality describe how power and inequality are organized across whole societies and across overlapping groups."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"Kimberle Crenshaw introduced intersectionality in 1989 to explain how courts sometimes failed women of color by treating race and sex as separate issues. The idea built on earlier work by thinkers such as the Combahee River Collective, who stressed overlapping sources of disadvantage. For gender roles and socialization, sociologists have long studied how children receive different toys, chores, and encouragement, and how these shape later choices. Patriarchy, as a concept, appears in the work of many feminist scholars who analyze how institutions distribute authority. Consider an example that uses all four. Imagine researchers studying who becomes a school principal. Gender roles may associate leadership with masculinity; socialization may steer girls and boys toward different ambitions; patriarchy may describe the historical concentration of authority in men; intersectionality reminds us that a Black woman and a white woman may face different barriers to the same job. Each concept highlights a different layer, and careful scholarship specifies which layer it is claiming to explain. Used loosely, these words can become slogans; used precisely, they generate testable questions about real patterns."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Define gender roles. 2) What is socialization, and name two settings where it happens. 3) In scholarship, does patriarchy describe individuals or a structure? 4) Who introduced the term intersectionality, and in what year? 5) Give an example showing why intersectionality matters. Answers: 1) Gender roles are shared social expectations about how people of a given gender should behave. 2) Socialization is the lifelong process of learning a society''s norms and roles; it happens in settings such as the family, school, peer groups, and media. 3) It describes a social structure in which power is concentrated in men, not an accusation against particular individuals. 4) Kimberle Crenshaw, in 1989. 5) For example, a working-class woman and a wealthy woman share a gender but face different constraints, so looking at gender alone misses part of the picture."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: using patriarchy to blame individual men rather than to describe a structure of power. Mistake: treating gender roles as fixed or universal; they vary by society and change over time. Mistake: reducing intersectionality to a simple list of identities instead of an analysis of how categories interact. Mistake: assuming socialization is finished in childhood; it continues throughout life. Exit ticket: (a) Which concept describes the lifelong learning of social norms? (b) Which concept describes the overlap of gender with race, class, and other categories? (c) True or false: patriarchy, in scholarship, is a claim about a social structure rather than about individual intentions. Answers: (a) socialization; (b) intersectionality; (c) true. Keep these four terms precise, and you will read the rest of the course with a sharper eye."}]}'::jsonb, 'These four concepts are tools, not slogans: each names a pattern researchers can observe and measure, and each can be applied carefully or loosely.', 13),
  (5, 'From women''s studies to gender studies: a short history', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to sketch how gender studies emerged, name its roots in women''s studies, and describe a few turning points in its development as an academic field. Warm-up, from the diagnostic: you identified women''s studies as the field from which gender studies grew most directly. This lesson fills in that story. History helps you understand why the field asks the questions it does and why its vocabulary changed over time. As you read, notice two things: first, how activism outside universities and scholarship inside them influenced each other; and second, how the name of the field shifted as its scope widened from a focus on women to a broader study of gender in all its forms. Dates and turning points are debated among historians of the field, so treat the outline below as a guide to major developments rather than a precise or complete chronology."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Gender studies grew most directly out of women''s studies, which took shape in universities in the late 1960s and 1970s. During that period, scholars and students argued that women''s lives, work, and contributions had been neglected across the curriculum. The first women''s studies programs and courses were established, new journals appeared, and researchers began to recover overlooked histories and to study inequality systematically. Over the following decades the field broadened. Scholars increasingly studied gender as a relation that shapes everyone, which led many programs to adopt the name gender studies, and some to add sexuality studies, queer studies, and masculinity studies. This widening reflected both new scholarship and critiques from within: writers of color, working-class writers, and others argued that early work had sometimes assumed a narrow experience and needed to attend to differences among women. The result was a more comparative and intersectional field. Today gender studies is institutionally established, with departments, degree programs, peer-reviewed journals, and professional associations in many countries, even as its exact boundaries and emphases continue to be discussed."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"Several works mark the field''s development. Simone de Beauvoir''s The Second Sex (1949) is often cited as a forerunner. The expansion of higher education and the social movements of the 1960s and 1970s created conditions for women''s studies to take root. Writers such as bell hooks argued that the field had to center the experiences of Black women and working-class women, not a single default. The Combahee River Collective''s statement (1977) stressed interlocking systems of disadvantage, anticipating later intersectional analysis. Judith Butler''s Gender Trouble (1990) influenced the turn toward studying gender and sexuality more broadly. An example of the name change illustrates the shift: a program founded as women''s studies might later be renamed gender studies to signal that it examines masculinity, femininity, and other categories together, and the relations among them. None of these milestones is uncontested; historians debate emphasis and timing. What is clear is a general movement from recovering women''s neglected place in scholarship toward a wider, comparative analysis of gender as a structuring feature of social life."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Answer from the reading. 1) From which field did gender studies grow most directly? 2) In roughly which decade were the first women''s studies programs established? 3) Name one reason many programs changed their name to gender studies. 4) Which 1977 statement is linked to early intersectional thinking? 5) Why did writers such as bell hooks push the field to change? Answers: 1) Women''s studies, which focused on recovering and analyzing women''s neglected place in scholarship. 2) The late 1960s and the 1970s. 3) To signal a broader focus on gender as a relation shaping everyone, including masculinity and other categories, rather than women alone. 4) The Combahee River Collective statement. 5) They argued that early scholarship sometimes assumed a narrow, default experience and needed to attend to differences of race and class among women."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: imagining the field appeared suddenly or from a single founder; it developed gradually from activism and scholarship together. Mistake: assuming the shift from women''s studies to gender studies erased attention to women; rather, it widened the frame while keeping that attention. Mistake: treating any one date as definitive; the history is debated. Mistake: picturing early feminist scholarship as uniform; it contained sharp internal debates, including critiques about race and class. Exit ticket: (a) Gender studies grew most directly from which field? (b) Name one force that helped women''s studies take root in the late 1960s and 1970s. (c) True or false: the move to the name gender studies meant the field stopped studying women. Answers: (a) women''s studies; (b) social movements and the expansion of higher education, among others; (c) false, since it broadened the scope rather than abandoning the earlier focus."}]}'::jsonb, 'A field''s history is itself contested: dates and turning points are debated, so treat this outline as a map of major developments, not a fixed timeline.', 12),
  (6, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"An interdisciplinary field is one that...","options":["refuses to use any methods","combines approaches from several disciplines","studies only one country","has no theory of its own"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Gender studies combines the questions, evidence, and methods of several disciplines."},{"prompt":"The claim that gender is socially constructed is best supported by...","options":["the fact that gender expectations vary across societies and over time","the fact that gravity is constant everywhere","a single personal anecdote","the arrangement of the periodic table"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Variation across cultures and eras is the strongest evidence that many gender expectations are produced socially."},{"prompt":"Socialization refers to...","options":["attending social events","the lifelong process by which people learn the norms and roles of their society","a government benefits program","an inherited genetic trait"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Socialization is the process of learning a society''s norms through family, school, peers, and media."},{"prompt":"Intersectionality was introduced to legal scholarship by...","options":["Charles Darwin","Kimberle Crenshaw","Isaac Newton","Adam Smith"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Kimberle Crenshaw introduced the term in 1989 to analyze overlapping categories such as race and sex."},{"prompt":"Women''s studies programs were first established in universities mainly during which period?","options":["the 1600s","the late 1960s and 1970s","the year 2010","ancient Rome"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The first programs took shape during the late 1960s and 1970s, the roots of today''s gender studies."},{"prompt":"The sociological sex and gender distinction is often credited in part to the work of...","options":["Ann Oakley","Marie Curie","Plato","no particular scholar"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Ann Oakley helped popularize the distinction in sociology in the 1970s."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (7, 'Theoretical approaches: feminist, queer, and masculinity studies', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to describe the main role of feminist theory in gender studies and to summarize, at an introductory level, two related approaches: queer theory and masculinity studies. You will also be able to explain why a field can hold several competing theories at once. Warm-up: recall from earlier lessons that gender studies is interdisciplinary and that scholars often disagree. Theory is where much of that disagreement becomes visible and productive. As you read, treat each approach as a lens that brings certain questions into focus while leaving others in the background. No single lens is complete, and part of becoming literate in the field is learning what each one is good for. Aim to finish the lesson able to say, in a sentence or two, what feminist theory, queer theory, and masculinity studies each tend to ask, and to recognize that there are several schools within each."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Feminist theory is the broad body of thought that analyzes gender inequality and the social position of women, and increasingly the workings of gender for everyone. It is not one theory but many: liberal feminism emphasizes equal rights and opportunities; socialist and materialist feminisms link gender to economic structures; and other strands stress culture, language, or the body. These schools share a concern with how gender shapes power, yet they differ sharply on causes and remedies. Queer theory, which drew on the work of scholars such as Judith Butler and Eve Kosofsky Sedgwick, questions fixed categories of gender and sexuality and studies how norms of normality are produced. It tends to ask how boundaries are drawn rather than to take them for granted. Masculinity studies, associated with scholars such as Raewyn Connell, examines manhood as something constructed and varied, including the idea of hegemonic masculinity, a dominant ideal that other forms are measured against. Together these approaches show the field''s range: it studies women, men, and the whole system of gender, and it hosts vigorous internal debate rather than a single orthodoxy."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"A few names map the terrain. Within feminist theory, writers from Mary Wollstonecraft in the eighteenth century to later thinkers such as bell hooks and Nancy Fraser represent very different strands. In queer theory, Judith Butler''s Gender Trouble (1990) and Eve Kosofsky Sedgwick''s Epistemology of the Closet (1990) are landmark texts. In masculinity studies, Raewyn Connell''s Masculinities (1995) is a standard reference, introducing hegemonic masculinity as an analytical tool. An example shows the approaches at work on one topic: advertising. A liberal feminist might ask whether women are shown in a full range of professional roles. A materialist feminist might ask who profits from particular images. A queer theorist might ask how an advertisement polices the boundary between acceptable and unacceptable gender expression. A masculinity scholar might ask which ideal of manhood the advertisement rewards. Each reading is partial, and together they are richer than any one alone. This is why the field values theoretical pluralism: competing frameworks, tested against evidence, tend to reveal more than a single framework applied everywhere."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Is feminist theory a single theory or many? 2) Name two strands of feminist theory and one difference between them. 3) What kinds of questions does queer theory tend to ask? 4) What does hegemonic masculinity refer to, and who is associated with the term? 5) Why might a field value having several competing theories? Answers: 1) Many, since it is a family of related theories rather than one. 2) For example, liberal feminism emphasizes equal rights and opportunities, while socialist or materialist feminism links gender to economic structures; they differ on causes and remedies. 3) It tends to ask how categories and norms of normality are produced and policed, rather than taking them as given. 4) A dominant cultural ideal of manhood against which other forms are measured; it is associated with Raewyn Connell. 5) Because competing frameworks, tested against evidence, often reveal more together than any single framework used alone."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating feminist theory as one fixed position; it contains several schools that disagree. Mistake: assuming queer theory and masculinity studies compete with feminist theory rather than extending the shared study of gender. Mistake: using hegemonic masculinity to mean all men or the worst men; it names a dominant ideal, not a group of people. Mistake: expecting a field to speak with one voice; sustained disagreement is a sign of health, not confusion. Exit ticket: (a) Name one strand of feminist theory. (b) What does queer theory tend to question? (c) Who introduced the concept of hegemonic masculinity? Answers: (a) for example, liberal, socialist or materialist, or culturally focused feminism; (b) fixed categories of gender and sexuality and how norms are produced; (c) Raewyn Connell."}]}'::jsonb, 'Theory here means a framework for asking questions and interpreting evidence; the field holds several frameworks that often disagree, which is normal in a healthy discipline.', 14),
  (8, 'Gender at work, at home, and in the media', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to explain how gender studies examines paid work, unpaid work in the family, and representation in the media, and to connect these to the division of labor. Warm-up, from the diagnostic: you saw that the field studies institutions, not only individuals. Work, family, and media are three such institutions where gender patterns are especially visible and well documented. As you read, keep two cautions in mind. First, patterns vary by country and change over time, so a claim true in one place and decade may not hold everywhere. Second, description is not destiny: showing that a pattern exists is not the same as saying it must continue. The goal is to give you a clear sense of the main concepts, namely the gendered division of labor, the double burden, and media representation, together with the kinds of evidence scholars use to study them."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The division of labor refers to how tasks are allocated among people, and the gendered division of labor refers to how societies have often assigned different work to women and men. Historically, many societies associated paid employment with men and unpaid domestic work and caregiving with women. Even as women''s participation in paid work rose dramatically over the twentieth century, research across many countries found that women often continued to do more unpaid housework and care, a pattern sometimes called the double burden or the second shift. Scholars also study occupational segregation, the tendency for women and men to cluster in different jobs, and gaps in pay and promotion. In the media, researchers analyze representation: who appears, in what roles, how often, and how they are portrayed. Studies have documented under-representation of women in certain roles and recurring stereotypes for several genders. These institutions interact: media images can reinforce expectations that shape choices at home and work, while changing workplaces can alter the images audiences expect. The field measures these patterns carefully and tracks how they shift over time and across places."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"The sociologist Arlie Hochschild''s book The Second Shift (1989) documented how many employed women did a further shift of housework and care at home, giving the pattern a lasting name. Researchers studying occupational segregation have tracked how fields such as nursing, teaching, engineering, and computing have been coded as feminine or masculine, and how those codings have changed. In media studies, content analyses count and classify portrayals; one widely discussed example is the Bechdel test, a simple measure asking whether a film includes two named women who talk to each other about something other than a man. The test is a rough indicator, not a verdict on quality, and scholars use many finer measures as well. Consider an example that links the institutions: if media repeatedly show caregiving as women''s work, young people may absorb that expectation, which can influence study and career choices, which in turn shapes who is available for which jobs. Gender studies traces such loops with data from surveys, time-use diaries, and systematic coding of media, always attentive to variation across places and years."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What does the gendered division of labor mean? 2) What is the double burden, or second shift? 3) What is occupational segregation? 4) Name one method used to study media representation. 5) Who wrote The Second Shift? Answers: 1) It is the pattern by which societies have assigned different work to women and men, often paid work to men and unpaid domestic work to women. 2) The tendency for employed women to perform a further round of unpaid housework and care at home. 3) The tendency for women and men to cluster in different occupations. 4) Content analysis, which counts and classifies portrayals; the Bechdel test is one simple example. 5) Arlie Hochschild. Note that all of these patterns vary across countries and change over time, so always ask when and where a figure comes from."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming the gendered division of labor is the same everywhere or fixed forever; it varies widely and has changed markedly. Mistake: reading a single statistic as the whole story without asking where and when it was measured. Mistake: treating the Bechdel test as a measure of a film''s quality rather than a rough indicator of one kind of representation. Mistake: confusing description with endorsement; naming a pattern does not approve of it. Exit ticket: (a) What term describes employed women doing a further shift of unpaid work at home? (b) What does occupational segregation describe? (c) True or false: patterns of gender and work are identical across all countries. Answers: (a) the double burden or second shift; (b) the clustering of women and men in different occupations; (c) false, since they vary by country and over time."}]}'::jsonb, 'When you read a statistic about gender and work or media, ask what is being measured, where, and when; patterns differ across countries and change over time.', 13),
  (9, 'Gender across cultures and how scholars study it', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to explain why gender studies compares societies, give examples of cultural variation in gender, describe several methods scholars use, and summarize a few contemporary debates in the field. This lesson draws the course together. Warm-up: recall the social construction argument from earlier, that variation across cultures and over time is strong evidence that many gender expectations are produced socially. Here we look at that variation directly and at the tools used to study it. As you read, hold two cautions. First, comparing cultures well means avoiding both the assumption that every society resembles your own and the opposite error of treating other societies as merely exotic. Second, debates in a living field are normal and are usually about evidence and interpretation. Aim to finish able to name a method, give an example of cross-cultural variation, and describe a current debate fairly from more than one side."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Across the world, societies organize gender in strikingly different ways, which is central to the field''s comparative method. The number of recognized gender categories, the tasks assigned to each, the ideals of beauty and conduct, and the rules of family life all vary. Some societies have long recognized social roles beyond a simple man and woman division. Kinship systems differ: some trace descent through mothers, others through fathers. These differences are evidence that gender arrangements are shaped by culture and history, not fixed by biology alone. To study all this, scholars use a range of methods. Qualitative methods include ethnography, in which a researcher observes and participates in a community over time, and in-depth interviews. Textual and content analysis examine documents, laws, and media. Quantitative methods include surveys, time-use studies, and the statistical analysis of large datasets. Historical methods use archives to trace change. Many projects combine several methods. Because gender touches sensitive areas of life, researchers also attend carefully to ethics: informed consent, privacy, and respect for the people studied. Good method, in this field as in any, means matching the tool to the question and being honest about limits."},{"heading":"Key thinkers & examples","text":"Early comparative work by the anthropologist Margaret Mead, in Sex and Temperament in Three Societies (1935), argued that temperaments linked to gender varied across the communities she studied; her conclusions were later debated, which itself illustrates how the field revises claims. Anthropologists have documented recognized third or additional gender roles in various societies, studied on their own cultural terms. For methods, a time-use study might reveal how many hours each gender spends on care work in a given country, while an ethnography might explain the meanings people attach to that work. Contemporary debates are lively and should be represented fairly. Scholars discuss how much of gender is shaped by biology versus society and how the two interact; how best to define key terms; how to study gender identity; and how findings should inform policy. On many of these questions reasonable researchers disagree, and the field advances by argument and evidence rather than by decree. A careful student learns to state each side of a debate accurately before reaching a view, and to distinguish well-supported findings from contested interpretations."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Why does gender studies compare different societies? 2) Name one qualitative and one quantitative method used in the field. 3) What did Margaret Mead''s early work suggest, and how was it received? 4) Give one example of cross-cultural variation in gender. 5) Name one contemporary debate and state it fairly. Answers: 1) Because variation across societies is strong evidence about how gender is shaped by culture and history, and comparison guards against assuming one society is universal. 2) Qualitative: ethnography or interviews; quantitative: surveys, time-use studies, or statistical analysis. 3) It suggested that gender-linked temperaments varied across societies; her conclusions were influential but later debated. 4) For example, societies differ in recognized gender categories or in how family descent is traced. 5) For example, how much of gender reflects biology versus society, a question on which researchers hold a range of well-argued positions."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming the arrangements of your own society are universal or natural. Mistake: treating other cultures as curiosities rather than describing them on their own terms with good evidence. Mistake: presenting one side of a live debate as settled fact; a careful student represents more than one position fairly. Mistake: confusing a well-supported finding with a contested interpretation. Exit ticket: (a) Name one method used to study gender across cultures. (b) Why is cross-cultural variation important evidence for the field? (c) True or false: on contested questions, gender studies advances by argument and evidence rather than by decree. Answers: (a) ethnography, interviews, surveys, time-use studies, or archival research, among others; (b) it shows that many gender arrangements are shaped by culture and history rather than fixed by biology alone; (c) true. You have now seen what the field studies, how it studies it, and how it handles disagreement."}]}'::jsonb, 'Comparing cultures requires care: avoid both assuming every society is like your own and treating other societies as exotic; aim to describe each on its own terms with good evidence.', 14),
  (10, 'Key terms', 'vocab', '{"items":[{"term":"gender studies","translation":"the interdisciplinary academic field that examines how gender shapes individuals, institutions, and whole societies","example":"A gender studies course might combine history, sociology, and literary analysis to study one question."},{"term":"sex","translation":"in common academic usage, the biological characteristics such as chromosomes, hormones, and anatomy","example":"In this usage, sex refers to biology, while gender refers to social roles and identities."},{"term":"gender","translation":"the social roles, behaviors, expectations, and identities that societies attach to categories such as woman and man","example":"Expectations about gender differ across cultures and change over time."},{"term":"social construction","translation":"the idea that many categories and expectations are produced and maintained by societies rather than fixed by nature","example":"That colors coded as masculine or feminine have changed is evidence of social construction."},{"term":"gender role","translation":"a shared social expectation about how people of a given gender should behave, dress, work, or feel","example":"The idea that leadership is masculine is a gender role, not a biological fact."},{"term":"socialization","translation":"the lifelong process by which people learn the norms and roles of their society","example":"Children are socialized through family, school, peers, and media."},{"term":"patriarchy","translation":"a social system in which power, authority, and resources are concentrated in the hands of men","example":"Scholars use patriarchy to describe a structure, not to accuse individuals."},{"term":"intersectionality","translation":"the analysis of how gender overlaps with race, class, and other categories to shape distinct experiences","example":"Introduced by Kimberle Crenshaw, intersectionality explains why gender alone can miss part of the picture."}]}'::jsonb, null, 9)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0005-0000-4000-8000-000000000027' and l.position = v.pos
);
